diff --git c/changelog.in w/changelog.in
--- c/changelog.in
+++ w/changelog.in
@@ -100,6 +100,35 @@ Delete the copy assignment of Home and of Space. Assigning one Home to
 another copy-assigned the whole Space that the home refers to. A Home never
 changes its space; construct a new Home instead. To copy a Space, clone it.
 
+[ENTRY]
+Module: kernel
+What:   change
+Rank:   minor
+[DESCRIPTION]
+Default special members that only construct, copy, or destroy members, and
+delete with = delete the members that must not be called. Across the
+library, default constructors, copy constructors, copy assignments, and
+destructors with empty or member-wise bodies are now defaulted on their first
+declaration; among them are those of the variables, most views, FloatVal,
+IntSet, TupleSet, DFA, Action, CHB, the argument arrays, and the range and
+value iterators. Behaviour is unchanged, but the ABI changes: several
+members that the libraries exported, such as the copy constructors of
+TupleSet, Action, and CHB, are now inline.
+[MORE]
+Copy members that were private and undefined, such as those of Search::Base,
+Support::BitSetBase, Set::GLBndSet, and FlatZinc::Printer, are now public
+and deleted, so copying one is reported as a call to a deleted function.
+
+A defaulted default constructor is no longer user-provided. A class with
+such a constructor and a field without an initializer can no longer be
+default-initialized as a const object: `const Gecode::IntSetRanges r;` no
+longer compiles. Initialize the object, or drop the const. This affects
+most range and value iterators: IntSetRanges, IntSetValues, IntVarValues,
+SetVarGlbRanges, SetVarLubRanges, SetVarUnknownRanges, the iterators in
+Iter::Ranges and Iter::Values, and the range and value iterators of views
+such as Int::ViewRanges and Set::UnknownRanges. It also affects SetRel,
+FreeList, Float::OffsetView, and Search::TraceRecorder::ID.
+
 [ENTRY]
 Module: support
 What:   removed
diff --git c/docs/code-style.md w/docs/code-style.md
index e570acaab8..f217967e45 100644
--- c/docs/code-style.md
+++ w/docs/code-style.md
@@ -138,6 +138,29 @@ strings, and callbacks. Their copies touch a reference count or allocate, so
 they follow the rule above. `FloatVal` is not exempt either: it is two
 doubles, not a handle.
 
+Special members follow the rule of zero: a class whose members copy, move,
+and destroy themselves declares no copy member, move member, or destructor.
+Where a special member must be declared, for example a default constructor
+beside other constructors or a virtual destructor, write `= default` instead
+of an empty body or a member-wise copy, on the member's first declaration in
+the class. A member defaulted out of line is user-provided and keeps the
+class non-trivial. Virtual destructors may stay out of line: their class is
+never trivial, and one defined in a .cpp file decides where the vtable is
+emitted. A member that must not be called is a public `= delete`, not a
+private member without a definition. `modernize-use-equals-default` and
+`modernize-use-equals-delete` enforce these rules.
+
+Some hand-written members stay, each after a
+`// NOLINTNEXTLINE(modernize-use-equals-default)` line. Cloning constructors
+such as `Space(Space&)` are not copy members: they take their source by
+non-const reference because cloning writes forwarding pointers into it, and
+they update their variables with `update()`. An empty default constructor
+stays where defaulting it would change what value-initialization does:
+`T()`, `T{}`, and the `alloc<T>(n)` functions of spaces, regions, and the
+heap zero a class whose default constructor is not user-provided, while the
+empty constructor leaves fields without an initializer untouched. A
+constructor whose body is empty only in some configurations stays as well.
+
 Other performance checks need focused review: `performance-enum-size` changes
 layouts and ABI, and `performance-no-int-to-ptr` conflicts with deliberate
 pointer tagging. Review `noexcept` guarantees, destructor triviality, and math
